= Preface <preface>

This document is a compilation of texts on aspects of Italian rapier fencing.
Some are handouts, others documentation, yet others a way for me to work through a topic.
Most were written in preparation for classes taught at _Klingenspiel_#index[Klingenspiel] in Vienna.
Others contain my notes on a topic that I have been working on myself.
All of them are living documents.
Each time I teach a topic, I update the chapter with new drills, ideas, and notes.
For now, the material here is confined to 'rapier alone', as that has been my primary focus so far.
When I start working more intensively on rapier and dagger, that will begin to appear here too.

===== Style
#index-start("Style")
A fencer reading through this document will hopefully get the impression that, while it is not confined to the teachings of a particular fencing master, the approach described is stylistically coherent.
I do not yet have a succinct definition of that style, but I am looking for a style of Italian rapier fencing that:

- is at least as 'martial' as it is 'art'.
- is distinctly 'rapier', not 'heavy smallsword' or 'long sabre'.
- is based on a set of principles that are simultaneously derived from the Italian treatises and logically sound.
- has viable defensive and offensive options.
- treats the rapier as a 'thrust-and-cut' weapon the same ways as a sabre is a 'cut-and-thrust' weapon.
- integrates footwork, bladework, and body movement.
- is not linear.
- considers the empty hand (the 'other strong hand') as an integral component of every action.
- not averse to using all parts of the rapier in offence and defence (pommel, quillons, etc.).

My criteria for integrating something into my fencing is that it can work in hard sparring, that is, against an opponent who is really trying to win.
#index-end("Style")

===== Using the Document
Although some chapters reference others,#footnote[These cross-references are hyperlinked in the PDF.] all are written to work as stand-alone documents.
The last chapter, @cues-and-details, contains the central ideas from the preceding chapters, condensed into coaching cues or descriptions of detail.
This is meant both as a quick reference for someone already familiar with the material, and as a starting point for someone looking for a particular topic.

The current version of this document, along with the source code and sections that are still in progress, can be found at #link("https://github.com/laugedideriksen/ItalianRapier").
